1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,000
French colony of Saint-Domingue produces immense
wealth through plantation slavery; social hierarchy

2
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:20,000
Enslaved Africans endure forced labor, surveillance,
and punishment under plantation discipline.

3
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:29,000
News of the French Revolution and rights debates
reaches the colony, heightening tensions among

4
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:38,000
Enslaved organizers build communication networks
across plantations in the northern plain.

5
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:47,000
At Bois Caïman in August 1791, leaders gather in a
secret ceremony linked in Haitian memory to

6
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:56,000
Plans are synchronized for a mass uprising in the
North Province.

7
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:06,000
In late August 1791, plantations burn and the
large-scale slave uprising begins.

8
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:16,000
Planters and colonial militias struggle to contain
coordinated attacks.

9
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:25,000
Rebel camps form with improvised command structures
and captured supplies.

10
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:34,000
Conflict expands into a multi-sided civil and
imperial war.

11
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:43,000
French commissioners arrive while local factions
contest authority and citizenship rights.

12
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:52,000
Free men of color and Black fighters organize armed
units amid shifting alliances.

13
00:01:52,000 --> 00:02:02,000
Spain and Britain exploit the crisis as war spreads
across the colony.

14
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:12,000
Toussaint Louverture rises as a disciplined
commander with operational skill.

15
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:21,000
Mounted scouts and small columns move through
mountain corridors and cane fields.

16
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:30,000
French commissioners in Saint-Domingue proclaim
emancipation in parts of the colony.

17
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:39,000
In 1794, revolutionary France abolishes slavery in
its colonies.

18
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:48,000
Toussaint aligns with Republican France after
abolition, redirecting his campaign.

19
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:58,000
Forces loyal to Toussaint push against Spanish
positions on Hispaniola.

20
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:08,000
Supply lines, powder, and food determine survival as
much as battle.

21
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:17,000
British expeditionary forces seize ports but face
entrenched resistance inland.

22
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:26,000
Tropical disease and attrition weaken European
campaigns.

23
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:35,000
Spain cedes Santo Domingo to France by treaty,
altering the strategic map.

24
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:44,000
Black and mixed-race officers expand command
responsibility in Republican ranks.

25
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:54,000
Toussaint centralizes authority and enforces
discipline in his army.

26
00:03:54,000 --> 00:04:04,000
Columns maneuver through sugar districts to isolate
enemy garrisons.

27
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:13,000
Britain begins withdrawing after years of costly
fighting.

28
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:22,000
Toussaint's administration extends control over most
of the colony.

29
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:31,000
Louverture issues the 1801 Constitution, asserting
autonomy while naming himself governor for life.

30
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:40,000
Toussaint occupies Santo Domingo and abolishes
slavery there.

31
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:50,000
Napoleon Bonaparte dispatches a major expedition
under General Leclerc to restore French authority.

32
00:04:50,000 --> 00:05:00,000
French fleets and troops land in early 1802 at key
coastal points.

33
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:09,000
Henri Christophe coordinates defensive withdrawals
and scorched-earth tactics in northern towns.

34
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:18,000
Retreating Haitian forces burn strategic sites
rather than surrender intact ports.

35
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:27,000
Haitian commanders shift to terrain advantage,
ambush, and fragmented fronts.

36
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:36,000
Heavy fighting at Crête-à-Pierrot demonstrates
tactical resilience against French assaults.

37
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:46,000
Leclerc combines military pressure with negotiated
promises to split commanders.

38
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:56,000
Toussaint agrees to retire after assurances of
safety.

39
00:05:56,000 --> 00:06:05,000
In June 1802, Toussaint Louverture is seized and
deported to France.

40
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:14,000
Toussaint is imprisoned in Fort de Joux in the Jura
mountains.

41
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:23,000
News and policy signals indicating restoration of
slavery reignite resistance.

42
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:32,000
Jean-Jacques Dessalines returns to open war against
French expeditionary forces.

43
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:42,000
Henri Christophe aligns in the renewed anti-French
campaign.

44
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:52,000
The struggle becomes existential: independence or
re-enslavement.

45
00:06:52,000 --> 00:07:01,000
General Leclerc dies as disease devastates French
ranks; Rochambeau assumes command.

46
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:10,000
French repression intensifies under Rochambeau,
hardening Haitian resolve.

47
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:19,000
Toussaint Louverture dies in captivity in April
1803.

48
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:28,000
Dessalines unifies forces under the Armée indigène
for decisive operations.

49
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:38,000
Haitian forces target forts and supply routes,
squeezing French coastal holdouts.

50
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:48,000
Former rivals coordinate under common command for
final victory.

51
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:57,000
Assaults intensify around Cap-Français and
surrounding strongpoints.

52
00:07:57,000 --> 00:08:06,000
Columns practice coordinated movement, volleys, and
bayonet charges.

53
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:15,000
Dessalines directs operations while sub-commanders
execute sector assaults.

54
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:24,000
Christophe leads organized troops in pressure
operations against French positions.

55
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:34,000
Both armies prepare around Vertières, a key
defensive line near Cap-Français.

56
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:44,000
On 18 November 1803, Haitian forces attack French
fortifications at Vertières.

57
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:53,000
Officers and infantry sustain repeated assaults
despite intense artillery fire.

58
00:08:53,000 --> 00:09:02,000
Coordinated Haitian pressure breaks French defensive
capacity.

59
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:11,000
The Battle of Vertières ends in Haitian victory,
decisive for independence.

60
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:20,000
Rochambeau agrees to evacuate remaining French
forces from Cap-Français.

61
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:30,000
Commanders transition from wartime coalition to
founding leadership.

62
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:40,000
Leaders gather in Gonaïves to formalize sovereignty.

63
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:49,000
On 1 January 1804, Dessalines proclaims the
independence of Haiti.

64
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:58,000
The new state adopts the name Haiti, drawn from the
island's Indigenous Taíno/Arawak heritage.

65
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:07,000
Commanders swear to defend freedom and prevent
return to colonial slavery.

66
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:16,000
Haiti emerges as the first independent Black
republic and a transformative event in Atlantic

67
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:26,000
The revolution's human cost is acknowledged: towns
destroyed, families torn, thousands dead.

68
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:36,000
Toussaint Louverture, Dessalines, and Henri
Christophe remain central figures of strategy and

69
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:45,000
The Haitian Revolution demonstrates that enslaved
people could defeat imperial armies and found a

70
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:54,000
Final credits list historical sources, archival
references, and production acknowledgements.
